{"kind":"assert","text":"Measurement before repair, and it changes what the repair is. The rule request db281ede asks me to define is already in main at e677dd8f, and the successors of that merge are not stale under it. What is stale is the binary doing the folding.\n\nEvidence. I re-folded the real log with main own code — go run ./cmd/gs status --repo . --json from a worktree at e677dd8f — and the three successors the automation published come out stale=false: 867e6897 at cmd/gs, f6dc6052 at docs, f99e1990 at internal/workroom. The resident at 127.0.0.1:7777 reports the same three as stale=true. bin/gs and the resident were both built at 14:24 on Aug 13; e677dd8f landed at 14:26 and later. So the run that failed, and the projection everyone has been reading since, predate the fix by two minutes of wall clock and one merge.\n\nWhy the fix already covers it. mergeSuccessionPlan carries a receipt signed plan to an artifact that merge actually published, publishedByMerge bounds who may borrow it, and stalenessCoveredByMergePlan walks the chain: successor to receipt to approval, and the approval bases are the promise 37d19b75, the review request 4fa203e3 and the three candidates 917dea7e, 85422ca6 and ce40a9b8. The three candidates are retired and in the plan, so they are covered directly; the promise and request are stale but not retired, and each recurses onto the primary candidate 917dea7e, which is in the plan. Every edge is covered, so the successor does not inherit the staleness. I also wrote the synthetic regression for the exact shape described in assert 4e3c0fe4 and it passes against main unmodified, which is the same result from the other direction.\n\nWhat I am not claiming. I have not proved that no shape reaches the failure — an approval whose promise or request chain reaches an artifact retired by some earlier merge, and therefore not in this plan, would not be covered, and that is a real gap worth a test. Condition 3 of the request says staleness through any edge other than the required-retirement chain must still propagate, which is exactly that case, so the two may need separating rather than fixing.\n\nWhat I think the repair now is. Rebuild bin/gs and restart the resident at main, then re-read; that is an operational act with a real blast radius while other actors are mid-flight, and it is not mine to do unilaterally. The three replacement successors codex published by hand, resting on the gitstore artifact at an unrelated path, are then unnecessary but harmless. If a code change is still wanted it is narrower than the request assumes, and I would want the gap above measured first rather than a second rule layered on one that already works.\n\nI have merged nothing, retired nothing, and not touched checkpoint 9e95cfa8."}